{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310598,"nonce":"MO3CRAhct2R+zGPLgBb+eVmO9II=","title":"A real defect in its own right, but NOT the CI 2x — that was the top-tier threshold. Kept as a record of a wrong diagnosis that shipped.","message":"**REVERTED — but this was NOT the CI regression.** Reverting it did not\nrecover binarytrees, fibonacci or nbody. The 2x was\n[[bugs_llvm_tier_stalls_to_compile]]: the top-tier threshold was lowered and\nthe LLVM compile is synchronous. This entry stays because the defect below is\nreal and the reasoning error is worth not repeating.\n\n**THE REASONING ERROR:** the pattern fit — deltablue improving while\ncall-heavy benchmarks doubled — and a fitting story was treated as a\nmeasurement. The regression was never reproduced locally before the fix for it\nshipped, which cost two CI cycles. The NUC could not reproduce it (6 GB), and\nthe Mac was never tried until after the second failure. **Reproduce first; a\nmechanism that explains the pattern is a hypothesis, not a cause.**\n\nCommit 00c0bb48 seeded Cranelift's `indirect_target_functions`\nover every module before declaring any of them, to make the two backends agree\non the hidden `env` parameter. That set is keyed by **`IrFunctionId`, which is\nmodule-local** — so reading every module up front marks ids that name a\n*different* function in a different module, and every one of those gets an\n`env` parameter it never needed. Each costs an argument push per call.\n\nThe CI numbers this was blamed for (binarytrees 179 → 371, fibonacci and\nnbody likewise, deltablue improving) belong to the threshold, not to this.\n\n**The pattern that MISLED:** deltablue is closure- and vtable-heavy, so\nmost of its functions were ALREADY in the indirect set — widening adds almost\nnothing there, and it instead gains from the statics fix (39e66be1). fib, nbody\nand binarytrees have few indirect targets, so the widening lands squarely on\nthe functions their hot loops call. Statics-heavy improved, call-heavy\nregressed. Precedent: eebd36be won **25% on fib** by removing ONE such push.\n\nThe lesson was already written down and walked past:\n[[bugs_irfunctionid_is_module_local]] — *never key a cross-module table by\n`IrFunctionId`; use names.* A name-keyed set is how to restore backend\nagreement if it is attempted again. Cranelift compiles every module anyway, so\nits set is complete by the end without seeding, and that is what the LLVM tier\nreads — the seeding only changed what was known at DECLARATION time.\n\n**Two verification failures that let this ship**, both the same shape — checking\nsomething narrower than CI and reading it as coverage:\n- Benchmarked only `rayzor-tiered` / `rayzor-cranelift` / `rayzor-llvm`, never\n  the `rayzor-precompiled*` or `rayzor-aot` rows CI reports.\n- Ran `cargo clippy -p compiler --lib` while CI runs\n  `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`.\n- The NUC **cannot** reproduce these numbers at all: 6 GB RAM means binarytrees\n  thrashes (600 ms–9.4 s there vs 179–370 ms in CI). Never validate an\n  allocation-heavy benchmark on that box.\n\nCaught before pushing, and worth remembering separately: making the vtable\ncounters `AtomicU64` for clippy put a `fetch_add` on EVERY virtual dispatch.\nThey are now gated behind `RAYZOR_VTABLE_STATS`.\n\nSee [[project_per_function_llvm_promotion]], [[bugs_statics_live_in_three_places]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_env_widening_2x_regression` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}